340095.statement:
Let $S_n(\!\raisebox{-0.3em}{
 \begin{tikzpicture}[line width=0.7pt, scale=0.15]
 \tikzstyle{disc} = [circle,thin,draw=black, minimum size=1.7pt, inner sep=0pt ]

 \draw[step=1, xshift=14pt, yshift=14pt, black!40, line cap=round] (0,0) grid (3,3);
 \draw[step=1, xshift=14pt, yshift=14pt, thick] (0,1) -- (3,1);
 \draw[step=1, xshift=14pt, yshift=14pt, thick] (1,0) -- (1,3);
 \foreach \x/\y in {1/1,2/3,3/2} \node[disc, fill=black] at (\x,\y) {};

 \end{tikzpicture}}
)$ denote the class of restricted permutations represented by the displayed pattern. For a permutation $\pi$, let $\operatorname{LRmax}(\pi)$ and $\operatorname{RLmax}(\pi)$ denote its numbers of left-to-right maxima and right-to-left maxima, respectively. Two statistics have symmetric joint distribution if the number of objects with prescribed values $(a,b)$ equals the number with values $(b,a)$.

**Jelínek's distribution conjecture.** For any $n$, $\operatorname{LRmax}$ and $\operatorname{RLmax}$ have symmetric joint distribution on $S_n(\!\raisebox{-0.3em}{
 \begin{tikzpicture}[line width=0.7pt, scale=0.15]
 \tikzstyle{disc} = [circle,thin,draw=black, minimum size=1.7pt, inner sep=0pt ]

 \draw[step=1, xshift=14pt, yshift=14pt, black!40, line cap=round] (0,0) grid (3,3);
 \draw[step=1, xshift=14pt, yshift=14pt, thick] (0,1) -- (3,1);
 \draw[step=1, xshift=14pt, yshift=14pt, thick] (1,0) -- (1,3);
 \foreach \x/\y in {1/1,2/3,3/2} \node[disc, fill=black] at (\x,\y) {};

 \end{tikzpicture}}
)$.

This is the weaker Conjecture 4.2 mentioned in the source. It follows from the stated symmetric joint distribution theorem for wNE-cells and first-row weight on Fishburn matrices together with the stronger bijection conjecture, but the source does not establish the bijection conjecture.
